.TH "ELEMENT.GETATTRIBUTE" "3JS" "August 19, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Element.getAttribute \- Element: getAttribute() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]getAttribute()\f[B]\f[R] method of the \f[CR]Element\f[R]
interface returns the string value of the specified attribute of the
specified element.
It returns \f[CR]null\f[R] if the element doesn\(cqt have an attribute
with the given name.
.PP
If you need to inspect the \f[CR]Attr\f[R] node\(cqs properties, you can
use the \f[CR]getAttributeNode()\f[R] method instead.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
getAttribute(attrName)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]attrName\f[R]
A string specifying the name of the attribute.
When called on an HTML element in a DOM flagged as an HTML document, the
name is normalized to lowercase.
.SS Return value
A string containing the attribute\(cqs value, or \f[CR]null\f[R] if the
element doesn\(cqt have an attribute with the given name.
.SH USAGE NOTES
.SS Decoded character references in attribute values
HTML character references in an attribute\(cqs source markup (for
example, \f[CR]&lt;\f[R], \f[CR]&amp;\f[R], or \f[CR]&#x3C;\f[R]) are
decoded by the HTML parser when the document is parsed, so
\f[CR]getAttribute()\f[R] returns the decoded value, not the original
source.
.PP
Given:
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqexample\(dq data\-payload=\(dq&lt;b&gt;hi&lt;/b&gt;\(dq></\f[B]div\f[R]>
.EE
.PP
calling
\f[CR]document.getElementById(\(dqexample\(dq).getAttribute(\(dqdata\-payload\(dq)\f[R]
returns the string \f[CR]\(dq<b>hi</b>\(dq\f[R].
.PP
Treating the return value from \f[CR]getAttribute()\f[R] as
already\-escaped HTML is unsafe.
If you read an attribute that holds untrusted data and then assign it to
\f[CR]innerHTML\f[R] or insert it into the document as markup, any HTML
references used to escape special characters will already be decoded,
and the result can be exploited for cross\-site scripting (XSS).
.PP
Use \f[CR]textContent\f[R] (or another text\-safe API) for untrusted
data instead of \f[CR]innerHTML\f[R].
.SS Retrieving nonce values
For security reasons, CSP nonces from non\-script sources, such as CSS
selectors and \f[CR].getAttribute(\(dqnonce\(dq)\f[R] calls, are hidden.
.PP
\f[B]Bad\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] nonce = script.getAttribute(\(dqnonce\(dq);
\f[I]// returns empty string\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Instead of retrieving the nonce from the content attribute, use the
\f[CR]nonce\f[R] property:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] nonce = script.nonce;
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
\f[I]<!\-\- example div in an HTML DOC \-\->\f[R]
<\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqdiv1\(dq>Hi Champ!</\f[B]div\f[R]>
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] div1 = document.getElementById(\(dqdiv1\(dq);
\f[I]// <div id=\(dqdiv1\(dq>Hi Champ!</div>\f[R]

\f[B]const\f[R] exampleAttr = div1.getAttribute(\(dqid\(dq);
\f[I]// \(dqdiv1\(dq\f[R]

\f[B]const\f[R] lang = div1.getAttribute(\(dqlang\(dq);
\f[I]// null\f[R]
.EE
